1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,944
Chapter three — a node is a model, not a box.

2
00:00:04,004 --> 00:00:07,547
This is the chapter that proves the thesis.

3
00:00:07,607 --> 00:00:11,424
Click any node on the canvas.

4
00:00:11,484 --> 00:00:17,297
The right-hand rail is
that node, top to bottom.

5
00:00:17,357 --> 00:00:23,990
A stream processor, version one —
not a rectangle with a label on it.

6
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:30,894
Every node carries its own call
graph, a domain expert and an Ask box.

7
00:00:30,954 --> 00:00:34,863
Basic is identity.

8
00:00:34,923 --> 00:00:45,477
Which domain it runs in — edge, on-prem, or a cloud.
That choice prices every link that crosses a boundary.

9
00:00:45,537 --> 00:00:50,617
Config is the physics of this component type.

10
00:00:50,677 --> 00:00:59,026
For a stream processor: window type, window
size, streaming units, watermark delay.

11
00:00:59,086 --> 00:01:06,027
The analysis panel says this node is the
bottleneck — its streaming units are saturated.

12
00:01:06,087 --> 00:01:09,858
So turn that dial.

13
00:01:09,918 --> 00:01:16,173
Six units to twelve.

14
00:01:16,233 --> 00:01:28,488
p50, p99, throughput and cost all move — on
the node, in the strip, and in the findings.

15
00:01:28,548 --> 00:01:34,045
Objectives are the targets
you are holding it to.

16
00:01:34,105 --> 00:01:43,644
Set one and it is checked against the
modelled metric — not against a hope.

17
00:01:43,704 --> 00:01:47,846
Data shape is the record
this node actually handles.

18
00:01:47,906 --> 00:01:57,512
Fields, types and sizes — this is
where the storage estimate comes from.

19
00:01:57,572 --> 00:02:02,714
Incoming load is the twenty-four
hour profile, not a single average.

20
00:02:02,774 --> 00:02:12,577
Daily total, peak per minute, and what
that peak is as a share of capacity.

21
00:02:12,637 --> 00:02:17,734
Network is exposure, port and protocol.

22
00:02:17,794 --> 00:02:28,172
Internal only, private endpoint, or public —
each one costs different latency and money.

23
00:02:28,232 --> 00:02:34,126
Host and disk is where a
lot of designs quietly fail.

24
00:02:34,186 --> 00:02:42,585
Watch the IOPS and throughput
as the disk changes.

25
00:02:42,645 --> 00:02:56,315
Network-attached storage: a different
ceiling, and often the thing that binds.

26
00:02:56,375 --> 00:03:04,378
Measured is your environment, not the model.

27
00:03:04,438 --> 00:03:17,566
Put a real number in and the model
defers to it over its own estimate.

28
00:03:17,626 --> 00:03:22,734
Resilience is inferred, not wished for.

29
00:03:22,794 --> 00:03:33,142
Pick replication and failover, and RPO, RTO
and rebuild time fall out of the choice.

30
00:03:33,202 --> 00:03:38,144
And Metrics is the answer the
whole rail was building towards.

31
00:03:38,204 --> 00:03:43,747
Latency split into compute and
disk. The binding constraint, named.

32
00:03:43,807 --> 00:03:55,751
The effective ceiling, the modelled
load against it, and the headroom left.

33
00:03:55,811 --> 00:03:59,754
Ten dials, a cost model
and a capacity ceiling.

34
00:03:59,814 --> 00:04:03,415
For every node on the canvas.
